% Command-Line Interface
% Chapter 6 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Command-Line Interface}

The command-line interface is the user's first point of contact with the
BitTorrent client. A well-designed CLI makes the tool intuitive to use while
providing flexibility through options. We use the [[clap]] crate with its
derive macros to define our argument structure declaratively, gaining automatic
help text generation, validation, and shell completion support.

\subsection{CLI Design Principles}

Our CLI follows common conventions for Unix command-line tools:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Required positional argument}: The torrent file path is the
  primary input and is required. Placing it as a positional argument makes
  the common case ([[literate-bittorrent file.torrent]]) simple.

\item \textbf{Sensible defaults}: Optional arguments have reasonable defaults
  that work for most use cases. The output directory defaults to the current
  directory, and the port defaults to 6881 (the traditional BitTorrent port).

\item \textbf{Short and long options}: Both forms are provided for frequently
  used options ([[{-}o]] and [[{-}{-}output]], [[{-}v]] and [[{-}{-}verbose]]).

\item \textbf{Helpful error messages}: Invalid input produces clear messages
  explaining what went wrong and how to fix it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Args Structure}

We define the CLI arguments using [[clap]]'s derive macros. Each field becomes
either a positional argument (no [[long]] or [[short]] attribute) or an option.
The [[Parser]] derive macro generates the parsing code, while [[#[command]]]
and [[#[arg]]] attributes configure behavior and documentation.

<<cli args>>=
use clap::Parser;
use std::path::PathBuf;

/// A pedagogical BitTorrent client implemented as a literate program.
///
/// Downloads files from the BitTorrent network using a torrent file or
/// magnet link. This client prioritizes clarity and educational value
/// over performance optimizations.
#[derive(Parser, Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[command(name = "literate-bittorrent")]
#[command(author, version, about, long_about = None)]
pub struct Args {
    /// Path to the .torrent file to download
    ///
    /// The torrent file contains metadata about the download including
    /// file names, sizes, piece hashes, and tracker URLs.
    #[arg(value_name = "TORRENT")]
    pub torrent: PathBuf,

    /// Output directory for downloaded files
    ///
    /// Files will be saved to this directory, creating it if necessary.
    /// Defaults to the current working directory.
    #[arg(short, long, value_name = "DIR", default_value = ".")]
    pub output: PathBuf,

    /// Listen port for incoming peer connections
    ///
    /// The client will listen on this port for connections from other
    /// peers. The default port 6881 is the traditional BitTorrent port.
    /// Ports 6881-6889 are commonly used.
    #[arg(short, long, default_value_t = 6881)]
    pub port: u16,

    /// Enable verbose (DEBUG level) logging
    ///
    /// When enabled, the client outputs detailed logging information
    /// useful for debugging connection issues, protocol exchanges,
    /// and download progress.
    #[arg(short, long)]
    pub verbose: bool,
}
@

The [[Args]] structure captures all command-line configuration. Let's examine
each field:

\begin{description}
\item[torrent] A required positional argument for the torrent file path.
  The [[value\_name = "TORRENT"]] makes the help text clearer by showing
  [[<TORRENT>]] rather than [[<TORRENT\_PATH>]].

\item[output] The output directory with [[{-}o]] short form and [[{-}{-}output]]
  long form. The [[default\_value = "."]] means files are saved in the current
  directory if not specified.

\item[port] The listen port for peer connections. We use [[default\_value\_t]]
  (note the [[\_t]] suffix) because this is a typed default rather than a
  string that needs parsing.

\item[verbose] A boolean flag that enables DEBUG-level logging. Flags don't
  take values; their presence sets the field to [[true]].
\end{description}

\subsection{Argument Parsing}

The [[parse]] method provided by the [[Parser]] trait handles argument parsing.
We wrap this in a custom function to make testing easier and to provide a
clean interface for the main program.

<<cli parse>>=
impl Args {
    /// Parse command-line arguments from the process arguments.
    ///
    /// This is the primary entry point for CLI parsing. It reads from
    /// `std::env::args()` and exits the process with a helpful error
    /// message if parsing fails.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```no_run
    /// use literate_bittorrent::cli::Args;
    ///
    /// let args = Args::parse_args();
    /// println!("Downloading: {:?}", args.torrent);
    /// ```
    pub fn parse_args() -> Self {
        Args::parse()
    }

    /// Parse arguments from an iterator of strings.
    ///
    /// This is useful for testing or when embedding the CLI in another
    /// program. The first element should be the program name (it will
    /// be used in error messages).
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::cli::Args;
    /// use std::path::PathBuf;
    ///
    /// let args = Args::try_parse_from([
    ///     "literate-bittorrent",
    ///     "test.torrent",
    ///     "-o", "/tmp/downloads",
    /// ]).unwrap();
    ///
    /// assert_eq!(args.torrent, PathBuf::from("test.torrent"));
    /// assert_eq!(args.output, PathBuf::from("/tmp/downloads"));
    /// ```
    pub fn try_parse_from<I, T>(iter: I) -> Result<Self, clap::Error>
    where
        I: IntoIterator<Item = T>,
        T: Into<std::ffi::OsString> + Clone,
    {
        <Args as Parser>::try_parse_from(iter)
    }
}
@

We provide two parsing methods:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[parse\_args]] reads from the actual command line and exits on error.
  This is what [[main]] will call.

\item [[try\_parse\_from]] accepts an iterator of strings, allowing tests to
  provide synthetic arguments. It returns a [[Result]] instead of exiting,
  making it suitable for testing error cases.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Input Validation}

While [[clap]] handles syntactic validation---ensuring arguments parse
correctly---semantic validation requires additional logic. The \emph{fail-fast}
principle demands we validate inputs thoroughly before beginning any expensive
operations. A user who misspells a torrent file path should learn of their
error immediately, not after attempting to parse corrupt data or failing
to connect to peers.

Our validation strategy addresses three categories:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{File existence}: The torrent file must exist and be readable.
  This catches typos early and provides a clear error message including the
  exact path that failed.

\item \textbf{Directory preparation}: The output directory may not exist yet.
  We attempt to create it (including parent directories) rather than failing.
  However, permission denied errors require clear reporting.

\item \textbf{Port validity}: While [[clap]] ensures the port fits in a [[u16]],
  we check for port zero (invalid for binding) and warn about privileged
  ports below 1024 that typically require root access.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Validation Errors}

We define a dedicated error type for validation failures. Each variant carries
the specific value that failed validation, enabling precise error messages.
The [[thiserror]] crate generates [[Display]] implementations from our
[[#[error]]] attributes.

<<cli validation>>=
use std::io;
use thiserror::Error;

/// Errors that can occur during argument validation.
///
/// These errors represent semantic problems with the arguments that
/// syntactic parsing (by clap) cannot catch. Each variant includes
/// the problematic value for clear error reporting.
#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum ValidationError {
    /// The specified torrent file does not exist.
    #[error("Torrent file not found: {path}")]
    TorrentNotFound {
        /// The path that was checked
        path: PathBuf,
    },

    /// The torrent file exists but cannot be read.
    #[error("Cannot read torrent file '{path}': {reason}")]
    TorrentNotReadable {
        /// The path that failed
        path: PathBuf,
        /// Description of why reading failed
        reason: String,
    },

    /// The output directory cannot be created or accessed.
    #[error("Cannot create output directory '{path}': {reason}")]
    OutputDirectoryError {
        /// The directory path that failed
        path: PathBuf,
        /// Description of the failure
        reason: String,
    },

    /// The port number is invalid (zero is not bindable).
    #[error("Invalid port number: {port} (must be 1-65535)")]
    InvalidPort {
        /// The invalid port value
        port: u16,
    },
}

/// A warning that does not prevent operation but should be shown to the user.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ValidationWarning {
    /// Port below 1024 typically requires root/administrator privileges.
    PrivilegedPort {
        /// The privileged port number
        port: u16,
    },
}

impl std::fmt::Display for ValidationWarning {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        match self {
            ValidationWarning::PrivilegedPort { port } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "Warning: port {} is a privileged port (below 1024) and may require root privileges",
                    port
                )
            }
        }
    }
}
@

The separation between [[ValidationError]] (fatal) and [[ValidationWarning]]
(informational) allows the caller to decide how to handle non-fatal issues.
A warning about privileged ports might be ignored by a root user but is
valuable feedback for a regular user wondering why binding failed.

\subsubsection{The Validation Function}

The [[validate\_args]] function performs all semantic checks in sequence.
It returns early on the first error (fail-fast), but collects all warnings
so the user sees the complete picture.

<<cli validation>>=
/// Result of argument validation.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ValidationResult {
    /// Warnings that were generated (empty if none).
    pub warnings: Vec<ValidationWarning>,
}

/// Validate command-line arguments semantically.
///
/// This function checks that:
/// 1. The torrent file exists and is readable
/// 2. The output directory exists or can be created
/// 3. The port number is valid for binding
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `args` - The parsed command-line arguments
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(ValidationResult)` - Validation passed, possibly with warnings
/// * `Err(ValidationError)` - A fatal validation error occurred
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use literate_bittorrent::cli::{Args, validate_args};
///
/// let args = Args::try_parse_from([
///     "literate-bittorrent",
///     "ubuntu.torrent",
/// ]).unwrap();
///
/// match validate_args(&args) {
///     Ok(result) => {
///         for warning in &result.warnings {
///             eprintln!("{}", warning);
///         }
///     }
///     Err(e) => {
///         eprintln!("Error: {}", e);
///         std::process::exit(1);
///     }
/// }
/// ```
pub fn validate_args(args: &Args) -> Result<ValidationResult, ValidationError> {
    let mut warnings = Vec::new();

    // Check torrent file exists
    validate_torrent_file(&args.torrent)?;

    // Ensure output directory exists or can be created
    validate_output_directory(&args.output)?;

    // Check port validity
    validate_port(args.port, &mut warnings)?;

    Ok(ValidationResult { warnings })
}
@

We decompose validation into focused helper functions. This makes the code
easier to test and understand---each function has a single responsibility.

\subsubsection{Torrent File Validation}

The torrent file validation uses [[std::fs::metadata]] to check existence
without reading the entire file. We also verify readability by checking
file permissions.

<<cli validation>>=
/// Validate that the torrent file exists and is readable.
fn validate_torrent_file(path: &PathBuf) -> Result<(), ValidationError> {
    // Check if file exists
    if !path.exists() {
        return Err(ValidationError::TorrentNotFound { path: path.clone() });
    }

    // Check if we can read the file metadata (tests accessibility)
    match std::fs::metadata(path) {
        Ok(metadata) => {
            // Check it's actually a file, not a directory
            if !metadata.is_file() {
                return Err(ValidationError::TorrentNotReadable {
                    path: path.clone(),
                    reason: "path is a directory, not a file".to_string(),
                });
            }
            // Try to open the file to verify read permission
            if let Err(e) = std::fs::File::open(path) {
                return Err(ValidationError::TorrentNotReadable {
                    path: path.clone(),
                    reason: e.to_string(),
                });
            }
        }
        Err(e) => {
            return Err(ValidationError::TorrentNotReadable {
                path: path.clone(),
                reason: e.to_string(),
            });
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
@

Note that we attempt to actually open the file rather than just checking
[[metadata.permissions()]]. On Unix, the permission bits indicate intended
access, but the actual ability to read depends on user ID, group membership,
and potential access control lists. Opening the file is the definitive test.

\subsubsection{Output Directory Validation}

For the output directory, we take a proactive approach: if it doesn't exist,
we create it. This is more user-friendly than requiring manual directory
creation. We use [[create\_dir\_all]] to handle nested paths like
[[/tmp/downloads/movies/2024]].

<<cli validation>>=
/// Validate the output directory exists or can be created.
fn validate_output_directory(path: &PathBuf) -> Result<(), ValidationError> {
    // If directory already exists, verify we can write to it
    if path.exists() {
        // Check it's actually a directory
        if !path.is_dir() {
            return Err(ValidationError::OutputDirectoryError {
                path: path.clone(),
                reason: "path exists but is not a directory".to_string(),
            });
        }

        // Try to verify write permission by checking if we can create a temp file
        // We use a deterministic but unique-ish name to avoid conflicts
        let test_file = path.join(".literate_bittorrent_write_test");
        match std::fs::File::create(&test_file) {
            Ok(_) => {
                // Clean up the test file
                let _ = std::fs::remove_file(&test_file);
            }
            Err(e) => {
                return Err(ValidationError::OutputDirectoryError {
                    path: path.clone(),
                    reason: format!("permission denied: {}", e),
                });
            }
        }

        return Ok(());
    }

    // Directory doesn't exist, try to create it
    match std::fs::create_dir_all(path) {
        Ok(()) => Ok(()),
        Err(e) => Err(ValidationError::OutputDirectoryError {
            path: path.clone(),
            reason: match e.kind() {
                io::ErrorKind::PermissionDenied => {
                    format!("permission denied: {}", e)
                }
                _ => e.to_string(),
            },
        }),
    }
}
@

The write test creates a temporary file to verify we have actual write
permission, not just apparent permission from the metadata. This catches
scenarios like read-only filesystems mounted with permissive mode bits.

\subsubsection{Port Validation}

Port validation catches the edge case of port zero, which is syntactically
valid for a [[u16]] but cannot be bound. We also generate a warning for
privileged ports (below 1024), which typically require root access on
Unix-like systems.

<<cli validation>>=
/// Validate the port number is usable for binding.
fn validate_port(port: u16, warnings: &mut Vec<ValidationWarning>) -> Result<(), ValidationError> {
    // Port 0 is special (OS assigns a random port) but not useful for BitTorrent
    // where peers need to know your port to connect back
    if port == 0 {
        return Err(ValidationError::InvalidPort { port });
    }

    // Warn about privileged ports
    if port < 1024 {
        warnings.push(ValidationWarning::PrivilegedPort { port });
    }

    Ok(())
}
@

We treat port zero as an error rather than a warning because BitTorrent
clients need a stable, known port for incoming peer connections. While
operating systems allow binding to port zero (which assigns an ephemeral
port), this is useless for a BitTorrent client that needs to announce
its port to the tracker.

\subsection{Logging Initialization}

Observability is essential for debugging protocol issues and understanding
download progress. We use the [[tracing]] ecosystem, which provides structured
logging with levels, spans, and fields. The [[tracing-subscriber]] crate
configures how tracing events are formatted and filtered.

Our logging strategy is:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Default to INFO level}: Shows piece completions, peer connections,
  and progress updates without overwhelming detail.

\item \textbf{DEBUG with --verbose}: Enables detailed protocol logging including
  block requests, message parsing, and state transitions.

\item \textbf{RUST\_LOG override}: Power users can set fine-grained filtering
  via the environment variable, e.g., [[RUST\_LOG=literate\_bittorrent::pieces=debug]].

\item \textbf{Output to stderr}: Standard output is reserved for potential
  machine-readable output (JSON status, pipe to other tools). Logs go to stderr.
\end{enumerate}

<<cli logging>>=
use tracing_subscriber::{EnvFilter, fmt, prelude::*};

/// Initialize the logging subsystem.
///
/// This configures tracing-subscriber with:
/// - Human-readable output with timestamps
/// - Default level INFO (or DEBUG when verbose=true)
/// - RUST_LOG environment variable support for fine-grained control
/// - Output to stderr (stdout reserved for machine-readable output)
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `verbose` - When true, sets the default log level to DEBUG
///
/// # Environment Variables
///
/// * `RUST_LOG` - If set, overrides the default filter. See tracing_subscriber
///   documentation for filter syntax. Invalid values fall back to the default.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use literate_bittorrent::cli::init_logging;
///
/// // Normal usage: INFO level
/// init_logging(false);
///
/// // Verbose mode: DEBUG level
/// init_logging(true);
/// ```
pub fn init_logging(verbose: bool) {
    // Determine the default log level based on verbose flag
    let default_level = if verbose { "debug" } else { "info" };

    // Build the filter, respecting RUST_LOG if set.
    // If RUST_LOG is set but invalid, we fall back to the default.
    let filter = EnvFilter::try_from_default_env()
        .unwrap_or_else(|_| EnvFilter::new(default_level));

    // Configure the formatter layer:
    // - Timestamps for correlating events
    // - Colored output when terminal supports it
    // - Hierarchical span display for context
    // - Output to stderr (not stdout)
    let fmt_layer = fmt::layer()
        .with_target(true)
        .with_level(true)
        .with_thread_ids(false)
        .with_thread_names(false)
        .with_writer(std::io::stderr);

    // Build and install the subscriber
    tracing_subscriber::registry()
        .with(filter)
        .with(fmt_layer)
        .init();
}
@

The [[EnvFilter::try\_from\_default\_env()]] call reads the [[RUST\_LOG]]
environment variable. If it's not set or contains invalid syntax, we fall back
to our default filter. This ensures the application never crashes due to a
misconfigured environment variable---a common frustration for users.

The formatter is configured for human readability: timestamps help correlate
events during debugging, targets (module paths) help identify where messages
originated, and the hierarchical span display shows the context chain.

\subsection{Module Structure}

The CLI module exports the [[Args]] structure for argument parsing and the
[[init\_logging]] function for logging setup. The main program will:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Call [[Args::parse\_args()]] to get configuration
\item Call [[init\_logging(args.verbose)]] to initialize logging
\item Pass the relevant fields to the download logic
\end{enumerate}

